   "claim": "On gfx1200 (hipcc), KernelCost.traffic summed over a split class's batch kernels ranks n_batches in the order of the row's summed spill.",
   "mechanism": "KernelCost prices each value its sweep evicts, in IR statement order, against a fixed budget, so splitting a class -- which takes values out of every batch kernel's IR -- can only lower its traffic, and the sum falls with n_batches -- and is ZERO for any kernel whose IR peak fits the budget. hipcc's count is of registers spilled after its own scheduling and allocation, and its demand is not the IR's liveness: a batch kernel whose IR peak is 55 fp64 values (110 VGPRs; L 10 b 6) still sits at the 192-VGPR ceiling and spills 377 registers (row raw, per kernel). So a batch kernel's spill shrinks far more slowly than its IR peak, and the SUM grows with the NUMBER of kernels. The model has no term for that per-kernel floor, and no budget changes the direction: at 64, 96 or 127 values L 13 and L 16 stay reversed.",
